<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tenagino Probus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 270</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tenagino Probus was the Roman prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 270. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> sent her general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Zabdas</a> into Egypt, Probus, returning from operations against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, fought the Palmyrenes and at first drove them back, but was defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> (Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>) and took his own life, leaving Egypt in Palmyrene hands until 272.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenagino-probus.jpg" alt="Tenagino Probus" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArdadN · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_Africa.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing is known for certain of Probus's origins or early career. His family name, Tenagino, is rare: its only other recorded bearers appear in two inscriptions from northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which may point to that region without proving it. The name does show that his family held Roman citizenship before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Constitutio Antoniniana</a> of 212, and so may have been people of substance. Some evidence suggests long military service, and he was probably one of the professional soldiers who gained governorships and commands once reserved for senators.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first known post was as praeses of Numidia, commanding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-iii-augusta">Legio III Augusta</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambaesis">Lambaesis</a>, the only significant Roman force in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. An inscription from Lambaesis of 267-268, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a>, is generally taken to refer to him, and he may have been the province's first equestrian governor. Inscriptions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timgad">Thamugadi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuburbo-maius">Thuburbo Maius</a> name him as governor under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a>. Stories in the Historia Augusta of his suppressing an insurrection in Carthago and killing one Aradio in single combat are generally rejected.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a> then made him prefect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, a post of the highest trust, and sent him into Creta et <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> against the Marmaritae, nomads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marmarica">Marmarica</a>; a Greek inscription from Cyrene, then called Claudiopolis, celebrates the victory. He next led a naval campaign against Gothic pirates raiding the islands and the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, probably in concert with a force under Lucius Aurelius Marcianus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The fullest account of his end is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>. A pro-Palmyrene faction led by one Timagenes encouraged the invasion, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>'s general Septiminus Zabdas entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> with 70,000 men, defeated 50,000 loyalists and left a garrison of 5,000. Probus returned, expelled the garrison and beat Zabdas again with local forces and soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Following Timagenes to the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, his army was surprised by 2,000 men who had seized a nearby height; captured, he killed himself. The Historia Augusta calls him Probatus, and some of his deeds were credited by it to the future emperor Probus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tenagino Probus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After fighting with the Roman prefect <b>Tenagino Probus</b>, the Palmyrenes gained control of Alexandria and the province, making it part of the Palmyrene Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-egypt">Palmyrene Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
